      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(web-media): add timeoutMs option to WebMediaOptions for remote fetches",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`loadWebMedia` / `loadWebMediaRaw` already accept `readIdleTimeoutMs` (idle timeout between data chunks) but have no overall request timeout for remote HTTP fetches. A server that accepts the connection then hangs before sending any data will hold the download open indefinitely.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n`readRemoteMediaBuffer` (called internally by `loadWebMediaInternal`) already supports `timeoutMs` in its options. The shared helper simply wasn't exposing or passing it through.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCallers that pass `readIdleTimeoutMs` to `loadWebMedia` can now also pass `timeoutMs` to cap the total request duration. This is the shared infrastructure foundation for the timeout fix pattern Alix-007 is applying across channel extensions (telegram #103020, nodes-camera #103019, qqbot #103018).\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `WebMediaOptions` type: added `timeoutMs?: number`\n- `loadWebMediaInternal` destructures `timeoutMs` from options and passes it to `readRemoteMediaBuffer`\n- No default value — callers opt in, so existing behavior is unchanged\n\nChange: 4 insertions, 0 deletions across 1 file.\n",
        "branch": "fix/web-media-fetch-timeout",
